From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] drm: a733: dp: chain a HPD bridge on the Type-C receptacle

The connector never says `connected`. Measured on the 8 GiB board,
2026-08-22, all on one boot: Type-C alt mode entered with `pin_assignment [C]`
and `hpd=1`, AUX up (`core 0x000a0509`), the sink answering DPCD `0x000 = 0x12`
and a full EDID block 0 over AUX -- and `/sys/class/drm/card0-DP-1/status`
reading `disconnected` for ever, `edid` 0 B, no modes, so `drm_client_setup()`
gives up at "Cannot find any crtc or sizes" and there is no /dev/fb0 and no
fbcon.

THE MECHANISM, AND IT IS ONE FWNODE COMPARISON
-----------------------------------------------

typec_displayport reports every HPD change with
drm_connector_oob_hotplug_event(dp->connector_fwnode, ...)
(drivers/usb/typec/altmodes/displayport.c:190, :214, :399, :827). That fwnode is
the TYPE-C PORT's:

    fwnode = dev_fwnode(alt->dev.parent->parent); /* typec_port fwnode */
    if (fwnode_property_present(fwnode, "displayport"))
            dp->connector_fwnode = fwnode_find_reference(fwnode, "displayport", 0);
    else
            dp->connector_fwnode = fwnode_handle_get(fwnode); /* embedded DP */
                                            -- displayport.c:800-804

and on this board that node is the `usb-c-connector`: tcpci.c:822 takes it with
device_get_named_child_node(dev, "connector") and hands it to TCPM as
tcpc.fwnode, and the Type-C class puts it on the port device verbatim
(drivers/usb/typec/class.c:2651, `port->dev.fwnode = cap->fwnode`).

drm_connector_oob_hotplug_event() looks the connector up by exactly that pointer
(drm_connector.c:3500-3514 -> drm_connector_find_by_fwnode(), :3464-3484) and
RETURNS SILENTLY when nothing matches -- no log line, no error, no counter.
connector->fwnode is set by drm_bridge_connector_init() from the LAST bridge in
the chain and from nothing else:

    if (drm_bridge_is_last(bridge) && bridge->of_node)
            connector->fwnode = fwnode_handle_get(of_fwnode_handle(bridge->of_node));
                                    -- drm_bridge_connector.c:758-759

The last bridge was ours, so connector->fwnode was `displayport@5740000`. Two
different nodes, one silent `return`. That is the whole defect: not a missing
interrupt, not a missing register, not a missing driver.

WHAT THIS ADDS
---------------

The mainline answer to precisely this, and it already has four in-tree users:
drm_aux_hpd_bridge -- a bridge with no registers whose of_node is whatever
device_node it was allocated against, whose type is
DRM_MODE_CONNECTOR_DisplayPort and whose only op is DRM_BRIDGE_OP_HPD
(drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/aux-hpd-bridge.c:171-194; the of_node comes back out of
platform_data at :183). sun60i_dp_next_bridge_get() walks this transmitter's
output port, and when the graph lands on a `usb-c-connector` it allocates one
against THAT node (devm_drm_dp_hpd_bridge_alloc/_add, aux-hpd-bridge.c:50-112)
and then resolves it back through the graph with devm_drm_of_get_bridge()
(bridge/panel.c:497-514). .attach chains it behind this bridge with
drm_bridge_attach(encoder, next, previous = ours, flags), so it -- not this
driver -- is last, and connector->fwnode becomes the receptacle's.

The shape is qcom_pmic_typec.c:91-115 (allocate the HPD bridge against the
`connector` child node, register it) plus msm/dp's chaining
(dp_display.c:1242 devm_drm_of_get_bridge on port 1 endpoint 0, attached after
its own bridge in dp_drm.c:352-358). Both halves are here; only the side that
performs them differs, and the next section says why.

WHAT THE VENDOR DOES INSTEAD, AND WHY THERE IS NOTHING TO PORT
---------------------------------------------------------------

The vendor's hot-plug path in
sunxi_drm_edp.c is extcon end to end and could not run on a mainline Type-C
stack at all:

| vendor | ours |
|---|---|
| hot-plug arrives as an extcon event: `extcon_get_edev_by_phandle(dev, 0)` in probe (sunxi_drm_edp.c:3406-3413), a notifier `edp_sink_extcon_evt()` (:401-426), `EXTCON_EDP_TYPE`/`EXTCON_DP_TYPE` both `EXTCON_DISP_DP` (:312-317) | drm_connector_oob_hotplug_event(), which drm_connector.c's own kerneldoc describes as being for "some USB Type-C setups where the hardware muxes the DisplayPort data and aux-lines but does not pass the altmode HPD status bit to the GPU's DP HPD pin" -- this board |
| that needs an extcon PRODUCER for `EXTCON_DISP_DP`. In 6.18.36 there are exactly two, `drivers/phy/rockchip/phy-rockchip-typec.c` and `drivers/extcon/extcon-usbc-cros-ec.c`; the whole of `drivers/usb/typec/` produces none (grepped, 2026-08-22). Porting the path would mean writing the producer as well | no producer needed: the event source is `typec_displayport.ko`, which this image already ships and which already fires on this board |
| it reaches into `"../drivers/extcon/extcon.h"` (:50-52) -- a subsystem's private header, from a DRM driver | public API only |
| the notifier does DPCD and EDID reads inline (`typec_dp_try_communication()`, :368-398) and, on failure, re-arms `typec_hpd_monitor` every second for up to 30 rounds into a front end whose bias is off | .hpd_notify opens the channel once and calls sun60i_dp_link_probe() once; the DRM core owns retries |
| a second, permanent `edp_running_thread` kthread runs alongside it, started and stopped by `edp_kthread_start()`/`edp_kthread_stop()` (:781-806) | no thread |

So there is nothing to port. The earlier instruction to port it, in this
driver's header block above sun60i_dp_bridge_link_budget(), is deleted rather
than struck through.

THE DECISIONS THAT ARE NOT STYLE
---------------------------------

1. THIS DRIVER PUBLISHES THE BRIDGE, NOT tcpci_rt1711h. Every in-tree caller of
   devm_drm_dp_hpd_bridge_alloc() is on the USB side (qcom_pmic_typec.c:95,
   drivers/soc/qcom/pmic_glink_altmode.c:464, ucsi_yoga_c630.c:265,
   ucsi_huawei_gaokun.c:427), so tcpci_rt1711h.c is the closest precedent and it
   is still the wrong place here. All four of those also DELIVER the event
   themselves through drm_aux_hpd_bridge_notify(); this board's delivery path is
   the generic alt-mode driver, which needs no Type-C-side driver at all -- only
   the fwnode. Registering there would buy nothing and cost two things: a DRM
   dependency in a shared USB driver on every rt1711h board in the world, none
   of which we can test (a change we cannot exercise is
   unproven) -- and, concretely, a probe dependency of the display pipe on the
   I2C TCPC. devm_drm_of_get_bridge() would then return -EPROBE_DEFER until
   tcpci_rt1711h had bound, so a PD or I2C failure would take the AUX channel,
   every debugfs bring-up verb and the whole pipe down with it. Publishing it
   here couples nothing: the receptacle's NODE is in the device tree whether or
   not any driver claims it.

   ⚠ The cost of that choice, named: if upstream ever gives tcpci an HPD bridge
   of its own, one of_node carries two and of_drm_find_bridge() returns
   whichever registered first. It is visible without hardware -- two
   `dp_hpd_bridge` auxiliary devices for one connector under
   /sys/bus/auxiliary/devices -- and the fix is to delete this half.

2. THE `displayport` PROPERTY IS REFUSED, NOT USED. displayport.c:801 would take
   `displayport = <&edp0>` on the receptacle and skip the whole chain. It is in
   the code and in NO binding: usb-connector.yaml is `unevaluatedProperties:
   false` and documents only an `altmodes/displayport` CHILD NODE, which is not
   a property; no device tree in 6.18.36 uses it. Writing it would be inventing
   a property, and the graph edge is the described way to say the same thing.

3. THE CONNECTOR BECOMES DRM_CONNECTOR_POLL_HPD, AND THAT IS THE POINT.
   drm_bridge_connector takes bridge_hpd from ANY bridge in the chain carrying
   DRM_BRIDGE_OP_HPD (drm_bridge_connector.c:678-679) and then sets POLL_HPD
   (:845-846) instead of POLL_CONNECT|POLL_DISCONNECT (:848). The chained bridge
   carries the op (aux-hpd-bridge.c:184), so the ten-second .detect poll stops.
   `drm-a733-dp-register-a-drm-bridge` recorded POLL_HPD as a hazard -- "waits for a drm_bridge_hpd_notify()
   that can never come" -- and that was exactly right while nothing could
   deliver one. What delivers it now is not drm_bridge_hpd_notify() at all:
   .oob_hotplug_event reaches drm_bridge_connector_handle_hpd() directly
   (drm_bridge_connector.c:170-177), which sets connector->status, calls
   .hpd_notify on every bridge in the chain (:135-147) and then sends the
   hotplug event (:161). This driver's own DRM_BRIDGE_OP_HPD is still NOT
   claimed and must not be: HPD_INPUT_STATE at 0x05740128 reads zero on this
   board through the VENDOR's live link (2026-08-19), so the pad is not routed.
   The comment block in sun60i-dp.c that asserted the old consequence is
   rewritten rather than annotated.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO
----------------------

⚠ A connector that reports `connected` does not put a picture on the screen.
The other defect measured the same day -- this hardware trains no link until the
pixel path is programmed, so `drm-a733-dp-register-a-drm-bridge`'s .atomic_enable has train and video in the
wrong order -- is untouched here and is fixed
separately. Nothing in this patch may be judged by /dev/fb0 or by a picture.

⚠ It also does not help a monitor that was already plugged in at boot with no
later event: POLL_HPD means nothing re-probes on a timer. That is not a
regression -- today's .detect returns `dpcd_valid`, which is false on such a
boot too -- and a re-plug fixes it. Named because it is the difference a
tester will notice.

⚠ And it introduces no probe ordering of its own, but the device-tree half
does: `remote-endpoint` is an fw_devlink supplier binding, so the new edge makes
this transmitter a consumer of the I2C Type-C controller. It is a mutual edge
and fw_devlink relaxes the cycle to permissive; the reasoning, the citations and
the boot-log observable are in `arm64-dts-a733-sbu-edge-from-the-dp-transmitter-to-the-typec-receptacle`'s header, where the edge is.

BEHAVIOUR WITHOUT THE DEVICE-TREE HALF
---------------------------------------

Nothing. of_graph_get_remote_node() finds no output port, next_bridge stays
NULL, .attach chains nothing, and the chain is exactly what `drm-a733-dp-register-a-drm-bridge` built. The two
patches are independent in that direction and only in that direction: `arm64-dts-a733-sbu-edge-from-the-dp-transmitter-to-the-typec-receptacle`
without this one adds a graph edge nobody walks.

WHAT IS VERIFIED
-----------------

✅ Applies at --fuzz=0 as part of the full series on a freshly unpacked 6.18.36.
✅ Every API used was read in this tree, not assumed: aux-hpd-bridge.c:50-112,
   :148-158, :171-194; drm_connector.c:3464-3514; drm_bridge_connector.c:135-181,
   :678-679, :758-759, :845-848; drm_bridge.c:1298-1385 and drm_bridge_attach()'s
   own unwind; bridge/panel.c:497-514; drm_of.c:240-289.
⬜ NOT HW-PROVEN. Nothing in it has run on silicon.

THE FALSIFIER
--------------

One observation, no debugfs verb involved: with the monitor plugged into J4,
`/sys/class/drm/card0-DP-1/status` reads `connected`; unplugging makes it read
`disconnected`; re-plugging makes it read `connected` again. Refuted by any of
those three not changing -- and specifically refuted, not merely unproven, if
`status` still never leaves `disconnected` while `/sys/class/typec/port0-partner/
port0-partner.0/hpd` reads 1. A `connected` that appears only after a debugfs
verb has been written is NOT this patch: that is `drm-a733-dp-register-a-drm-bridge`'s .detect returning
dpcd_valid, which is a separate defect with a separate fix.

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/Kconfig b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/Kconfig
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/Kconfig
@@ -13,6 +13,16 @@ config DRM_SUN60I_DP
 	# the same kind of accident that once held up the
 	# console chain. Selected explicitly so this driver stops depending on it.
 	select DRM_KMS_HELPER
+	# The out-of-band hot-plug path. drm_aux_hpd_bridge is what stands for
+	# the usb-c-connector node in the bridge chain, so that
+	# drm_bridge_connector_init() takes THAT node's fwnode -- the one
+	# dp_altmode_probe() looks the connector up by
+	# (drivers/usb/typec/altmodes/displayport.c:800). The symbol has no
+	# prompt (drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/Kconfig:25-31), so it can only be
+	# reached by a select; DRM_BRIDGE is def_bool y under DRM and this
+	# driver already depends on DRM && OF, so its own dependencies are met
+	# by construction and the select needs no condition.
+	select DRM_AUX_HPD_BRIDGE
 	select GENERIC_PHY
 	help
 	  Enable this for the DisplayPort transmitter of the Allwinner A733
